        "raw_text": "How To identify and avoid Traps in Trading: Understanding volume analysis in Stock Market trading Agree & Join LinkedIn By clicking Continue to join or sign in, you agree to LinkedIn’s User Agreement , Privacy Policy , and Cookie Policy . Sign in to view more content Create your free account or sign in to continue your search Email or phone Password Show Forgot password? Sign in Sign in with Email or New to LinkedIn? Join now By clicking Continue to join or sign in, you agree to LinkedIn’s User Agreement , Privacy Policy , and Cookie Policy . Skip to main content Investors who operate on the stock exchanges usually focus on the prices of shares. Although prices play a crucial role in the stock exchanges, they do not give a complete view of what is happening on the exchanges. One of the most critical aspects of stock exchanges is volume. It gives an idea of how actively a stock is traded on the exchanges and helps investors understand the strength of movements.  In this blog article on role of volume in trading and technical analysis, we will explore the critical relationship between volume and liquidity, what does trading volume indicate, the role volume based indicators like OBV and VPT that professional traders use to forecast movements, the role of volume in technical analysis and why these factors and other important ones are critical when making trading decisions. Let’s begin with. What Is Volume in the Stock Market? In the stock exchange market, volume is defined as the total number of shares traded over a particular period of time, usually on a single day. Volume is used to determine the number of shares for a particular company that is being traded among buyers and sellers. For example, let’s assume that 50,000 shares of a company are bought and sold on a particular day. In such a case, the volume for the company on that particular day is 50,000. Volume and Liquidity The volume has a direct relationship with the liquidity of the stock in the stock market. Liquidity refers to the ease with which shares can be bought or sold without causing any significant change in the price of the shares. The stocks with high trading volumes are said to be more liquid. This means that the shares can be easily bought or sold at the desired price because many investors are participating in the market. For example, the stocks of big companies listed on the stock exchanges tend to have high trading volumes. This means that the shares can be easily bought or sold. The stocks with low trading volumes tend to be less liquid.This means that the shares cannot be easily sold because there are few investors willing to buy the shares at the desired price. What Does Trading Volume Indicate? Trading volume is a significant indicator of various trends in the stock market. When both price and volume increase, it is a clear indicator that investors are confident about the company’s future performance. If there is an increase in volume and a corresponding decrease in price, it is a clear indicator that all the investors are selling their shares in large numbers. Role of Volume in Technical Analysis Technical analysis is often employed by traders to analyze price patterns and forecast future market movements. Volume is an integral part of technical analysis. Price trends are considered more reliable if they are accompanied by strong trading volume. For example, if a stock is trading above an important level called resistance and trading volume is high, it is considered a strong buy signal. Volume-Based Indicators Used by Traders Several technical indicators use volume data to help traders understand market behavior. These indicators combine price and volume to identify trends and trading opportunities. Daily Volume and Average Daily Volume Daily volume refers to the total number of shares traded on a stock within a given trading session. To be able to understand trading activity over a longer period of time, investors may use Average Daily Volume (ADV) as a tool. Average Daily Volume is used to measure the average number of shares traded over a given period of time,  20 days or one month. Using Average Daily Volume, an investor may be able to gauge the level of interest in a given stock. If the average volume is large, it means there is a lot of activity going on; otherwise, it is low. On-Balance Volume (OBV) OBV is a technical indicator commonly employed for the analysis of market trends. This indicator measures the buying and selling pressure of shares based on their price movements and volumes of shares traded. The idea behind the OBV indicator is to check whether the closing share price of a particular day is higher than the previous day’s closing share price. If the closing share price is higher, then the volume of shares traded is added to the OBV value. If the closing share price is lower, then the volume of shares traded is deducted from the OBV value. An increasing OBV value indicates that buying pressure is rising, whereas a decreasing OBV value indicates that selling pressure is rising. This can be compared to the trend of the share to check whether the trend is strong or weak. Recommended by LinkedIn Here Come the Earnings Oluwatosin Olaseinde 4 years ago Samuel’s Big Move Oluwatosin Olaseinde 5 years ago A Stock Up 476% on 23rd Dec 2025!?! - Some food for… Harish Chandan 8 months ago Volume Price Trend (VPT) The Volume Price Trend (VPT) indicator is used to measure the relationship between price variations and trading volume. It takes into account the percentage change in price and multiplies it by volume to assess trends in the market. The formula for VPT is: VPT = Previous VPT + Volume x (Today’s Closing Price – Previous Closing Price)/Previous Closing Price In cases where there is an increase in price and VPT, it shows that there is a strong rising trend in the market. On the other hand, if there is an increase in price but no corresponding increase in VPT, it may show a weak rising trend. Volume RSI The Relative Strength Index Volume works on the same principle as the conventional Relative Strength Index, except that the trading volume is used instead of the prices. This Relative Strength Index ranges between 0 and 100. When the Relative Strength Index crosses the 50-point mark, it indicates more buying activity in the market. When the Relative Strength Index falls below the 50-point mark, it indicates more selling activity in the market. Volume Bar Charts The volume may be shown on the charts as bar graphs located at the bottom of the stock price charts. These bars indicate the amount of trading activity during a given period of time. For instance, if the stock prices are rising and the volume bars are rising at the same time, then the movement of the stocks can be said to be strong and reliable. In conclusion, ‘Volume’ is one of the most critical parameters used in stock trading. It is defined as the total number of shares traded during a given period of time. It is an indicator of the participation level in a stock.Investors who study volume movements can gain insights into investor sentiment, strong trends in stock prices, and even stock liquidity. Stocks with higher volume indicate good investor interest, while stocks with low volume indicate low trading activity. Ticker MVT is the product of Ticker Data and is the cross-asset market data platform in the financial sector. It can be integrated to provide real-time streaming quotes, market analytics, and news with information on international and domestic exchanges and OTC markets. https://www.tickermarket.com/ . Ticker Data MVT is a great way to be prepared for your investing journey. Do your research and Happy Investing! The information on this blog is provided for information purposes only. It does not constitute an offer, recommendation, or solicitation to any person to enter into any transaction or adopt any hedging, trading, or investment strategy, nor does it constitute any pred