        "url": "https://tradethatswing.com/how-to-spot-trends-and-trend-reversals-using-price-action/?srsltid=AfmBOooiE2ULboS8Ij0CPo0aamAmcaNZvzc2_KKFvROXmH0QHb9wduCD",
        "raw_text": "How to Spot Trends and Trend Reversals Using Price Action - Trade That Swing $ 0.00 How to Spot Trends and Trend Reversals Using Price Action November 20, 2025 Posted by Cory Mitchell, CMT Technical Analysis 7 Comments Price action trading relies solely on the price chart for making trading decisions. Price movement is the purest indicator traders have. Price determines profit and loss, nothing else. Price action traders believe the price tells you everything you need to know. And it’s true. You don’t make money off fundamentals, off indicators, or off statistics. Money is only made based on price movements. If you buy and the price goes up, you have a potential profit there. It doesn’t matter if every indicator and every fundamental and economic figure says something should rise, if it doesn’t, and you bought it, there is no profit there. Price action is king/queen. The video below goes over the basics of trading price action. These concepts are not trading strategies. Strategies involve finding exact entry and stop loss points, as well as position sizing . Analyzing price action is noting how the price is acting so we can choose when and what strategies we want to implement. In the video, I use forex pairs in the examples, but the same concepts are applicable to trading stocks as well. Side note: Big news events can have a dramatic and quick effect on the price of any asset. Price action can’t tell us what the news will be. Therefore, I don’t hold day trades through any news event that could easily result in a large price movement through my stop loss . And I don’t hold stock swing trades through earnings announcements. How to Analyze Price Action The most basic concept of price action is that you wait for a move in a direction before acting. If the price is falling, it is falling. It doesn’t matter if you think it should go up. Falling is falling, and rising is rising. Similarly, sideways is sideways, until it breaks out and starts rising or falling. Price action traders usually require price confirmation for whatever they do. If they want to buy, they wait for signs that the price is starting to rise. Waiting for a huge rally is NOT required in this case. Just some evidence that the price is turning. Next, we need to understand trends. Trends are created because Trending waves are bigger than Correction waves. How do we know which waves are Trending and which waves are Corrections? By their relative size. Trending waves need to be bigger than Corrective waves, otherwise, no progress is made. When a Corrective wave becomes as large as the last Trending wave, that indicates a potential trend change or a sideways period. The former trend is in question because Corrective waves need to be smaller than Trending waves for a trend to exist. Charts from TradingView . The charts I use. In the chart above (and all charts), there are waves within waves. For example, the extended corrective wave has many small ups and downs within it. So the correction ends up having Trending and Corrective waves on a smaller scale than the large Trending wave that occurred before it. More details on this are discussed below. As the price chart progresses, the trend reverses because the down moves become bigger than the up moves. Each of these concepts is discussed in detail in the video. We constantly analyze the size of price waves to help assess the strength of the trend, when the trend is in question, and when the trend is reversing. Waves can also be dissected into what I call wiggles. They are the small oscillations within Trending and Corrective waves. These wiggles are also forming Trending and Corrective waves, but on a smaller scale. It is up to each trader to determine their own “scale” (this is different from the chart timeframe) by isolating wave sizes that are important to them. Waves that occur inside are wiggles. These wiggles can also be analyzed based on the size of their moves. If the trend is down (last Trending waves down), and we are correcting to the upside, we can watch for bigger moves down and smaller moves up in the wiggles. This helps indicate the correction is running out of steam and the downtrend may resume soon. Once the price starts dropping, the analysis is confirmed. If the trend is up (last Trending wave up), and we are correcting to the downside, watch for bigger moves up and smaller moves down in the wiggles. This indicates the downside correction is running out of steam and the uptrend may resume soon. The price must move to the upside to confirm the analysis. To aid in all this, we can connect swing high points to other swing high points and swing low points to other swing low points. By connecting highs to highs and lows to lows, we can see how the angle of a trend changes, and we can see the change in trend more clearly and visually. We also must remember that price action concepts can be applied on the large scale (looking at overall price waves) or the small scale (each wiggle). The stock chart below shows the overall concept of looking at the big scale. The chart above shows big green Trending up waves and smaller Corrective red waves. The triangle pattern on the right can confuse people. It may appear like the trend is over because the price is no longer making higher swing highs. Yet all these waves in the triangle are smaller than the last wave up. So the whole triangle is just a Corrective wave so far. If the wiggles to the upside within the triangle start getting bigger, that could indicate the buying pressure is starting to build (this will result in a breakout of the triangle). On the other hand, if the wiggles to the downside start getting bigger, that could indicate selling pressure is building (breakout to downside). Looking for more price action strategies and methods?  Check out the EURUSD Day Trading Course . It covers price action strategies for crushing the EURUSD in two hours or less per day, as well as mental game work and exercises to get in the profit-making zone and stay there. Applying Price Action Concepts to Trading Generally, we want to take trades in the direction of the Trending waves. This is the trend direction. Trading in the direction of the biggest waves provides the biggest profit potential…because the waves are bigger. Correction waves are smaller, leaving less room for profit. I may trade in both directions if the price is volatile, making sharp and large moves in both directions. The Trending waves may still be bigger, but in these conditions, Corrections may provide a lot of profit potential as well. Once we have established the trend direction, this is typically the direction we want to be trading in. The trend direction doesn’t mean that there is a beautiful easy-to-spot uptrend or downtrend, which is how most people think of trends. It really just means we have had at least one Trending wave (bigger wave) in one direction. We can then watch for opportunities to enter in that direction based on our strategies. We then watch the Corrective waves closely. Once the wiggles in the correction provide us with clues that the correction is losing momentum, we prepare to pounce. Once the price starts moving in the trending direction again, we enter a trade in the direction of the Trending wave. This is a complex concept to depict and explain in a single chart, so please see the video for numerous examples. There are also many ways we could potentially apply these concepts. Here is an example on a stock chart. A stop loss is placed just outside the correction low/high, or wherever the strategy being used dictates. See the Cup and Handle for an example of a stock trading strategy based on price action concepts. The EURUSD Session High Low Day Trading Strategy is also a price action-based method. This is where a strategy comes in. You can analyze the price action, but you need to define exactly what has to occur for a trade to trigger (the exact criteria that cause you to click the buy or sell button). If you start trading based on price action and you are always asking yourself, “Is this a valid trade?” you haven’t fine-tuned your strategy enough, and you need to get more precise. Practicing Price Action Analysis Practicing price action analysis takes time, but it isn’t difficult. The trick is being able to analyze what is going on in the moment. This way, no matter what the price does, you know what you are going to do (or not do). You are always thinking ahead. Open up a random chart. Scroll back in time. Draw lines on the big waves (Trending) and small waves (Corrective). Connect swing highs to highs and swing lows to lows. Our main goal is to assess which direction we want to trade, and what needs to happen to get us into a trade. Note the trend direction. This is our trade direction, usually. Monitor pullbacks/corrections for trading opportunities. The wiggles often help determine when a correction is running out of steam. Or simply consider what would confirm the price is moving back in the trending direction (to warrant a trade)? WE DON’T NEED TO TRADE EVERY PRICE WAVE! Only consider a trade (even when practicing) when there is price action evidence to suggest taking a trade. Small sideways periods, called contractions or consolidations, are a common trade trigger. When the price moves out of a consolidation or contraction in the direction of the Trending waves, there may be an opportunity. Be aware of the waves on a larger scale (big waves) and a smaller scale (wiggles). This doesn’t mean on a different time frame. I generally don’t monitor multiple time frames for my trades. On a single timeframe, you will have big waves and the gyrations within it. Always be considering: What would need to happen to cause a trend reversal? What has to happen to indicate the correction is over? What has to happen to trigger me into a trade? Practice this on your chart, unveiling price action by scrolling to the right one bar/candle at a time As you get better, try practicing on real-time price data in a demo account. False Breakouts False breakouts are important to the price action trader. Occasionally, you will be monitoring a corrective wave. Let’s say the trend is down and we are in a corrective upwave. The wiggles start to indicate the upward correction is topping out. We have gotten a larger wiggle to the downside, and smaller wiggles to the upside and the price is starting to drop again. Our strategy tells us to get short with a stop loss above the correction high. The price spikes up, stopping us out, but then quickly starts falling again. There is the option to short it again. USDJPY 5-Minute False breakouts happen. We sometimes get a short-term price burst in the opposite direction, but if it quickly fails, and everything still points to lower prices, we want to get back into that trade. Price action is still indicating more downside. Tied to the concept of the false breakout are false reversals. This is when the trend looks like it is going to reverse, but then quickly turns back in the trending direction. This video discusses that and was the basis for developing the Double Pump Day Trading Strategy . Price Action Still Produces Losing Trades Trading based on price action doesn’t mean we are always right. Actually, trading has nothing to do with being right or wrong. Trading is about following a strategy to make money over many trades. Any single trade result is inconsequential. Like any strategy, we are just waiting for signals that give us a profitable edge over many trades . We can’t control what the market does. All we can do is trade what comes. Sometimes we will have an amazing setup, and the price will go the other way. That doesn’t mean we were wrong in our analysis. That’s why we have a stop loss. PRICE IS NEVER SUPPOSED TO DO ANYTHING. IT JUST DOES WHAT IT DOES. We incorporate the new price moves in our analysis and proceed. Track what it does and act accordingly. Price action trading is about adaptability . Price moves may indicate a move down, but then we have a sharp move up. That can’t be predicted. And we don’t need to predict it. Money is made by cutting losses and riding the trades that do work out (bigger profit target or trailing stop loss ). When the price moves the other direction, that tells us something. Think about what it is telling you, objectively. Don’t think about winning or losing. Just think about which way the price waves are moving. Plan what to do (or not do) based on what transpires. Above all, if you want to get good at price action trading, you need to practice! Interested in trading price action patterns in stocks? Check out my Complete Method Stock Swing Trading Course . It’s all about how to find and trade explosive stocks, developing a method, and knowing when to be aggressive and when to hold back. By Cory Mitchell, CMT Disclaimer: Nothing in this article is personal investment advice, or advice to buy or sell anything. Trading is risky and can result in substantial losses, even more than deposited if using leverage. 7 Comments 88 About Cory Mitchell, CMT Cory is a professional trader since 2005. In between trading stocks and forex he consults for a number of prominent financial websites and enjoys an active lifestyle. He runs TradeThatSwing and coaches individual clients. You also might be interested in How to Draw and Update Horizontal Levels When Trading: Filter Low Quality Trades and Highlight High Quality Ones Dec 16, 2025 In this video, I walk through how to draw horizontal[...] Best Books on How to Swing Trade Stocks Feb 12, 2026 These are some of my favorite books for learning how[...] EURUSD Day Trading Strategy Review for April (+76R Potential, Less Commissions) May 2, 2021 This is how the EURUSD day trading strategies I am[...] 7 Comments Leave your reply. Akiko · Reply July 26, 2023 at 1:01 AM With regard to not holding stocks through earnings announcements – how do you set your rule? Is there any rule like “do not enter the position if the earnings announcements is coming in X days”? If you are holding a position of a stock for a while but the earnings announcement is coming soon, when do you sell? Cory Mitchell, CMT · Reply Author July 26, 2023 at 8:54 PM If I’m holding a position I get out the day before earnings, or if the price has just had a nice Spike and earnings are only a couple days away I may just close it out or put a very aggressive trailing stop loss on it to protect my profit. In terms of initiating a trade before earnings, there needs to be enough time for the trade to potentially hit its Target. By looking at recent price action we can determine how fast the price is moving, so I’d want to reasonably assume that the price could hit the target or get very close to it before earnings. We of course can never know for sure if it will hit the target before earnings but it should be a reasonable estimate. This article discusses https://tradethatswing.com/know-how-long-a-trade-could-last-before-entering/ Akiko · Reply July 27, 2023 at 3:39 PM Thank you very much for referring me to this article – it really helps. Recently my positions took so long to reach my target price, so even if I found attractive stocks, I couldn’t even place orders because I didn’t have any capital left! Because of this experiences, I started comparing Profit/Loss % vs Time in Trade (Days) for my previous trades, and noticed I should check not only R:R but also potential % profit before placing order. Russhane Bowerise · Reply April 12, 2023 at 11:03 PM Thanks Cory, I read this several months ago and gave it another read today. After practicing the other concepts in the course this is making much more sense. Identifying impulsive and corrective waves helps with the confirmation bias in trades. Boniface K · Reply October 2, 2022 at 5:26 PM Hi Cory, Do you also incorporate reading the tape with the price actions? If not, do you use it with your trading? If you do, can you tell how it can be added to enhance price actions when entering and exiting trades? Learning a lot from your articles. Thank you for doing this! Cory Mitchell, CMT · Reply Author October 2, 2022 at 6:01 PM No, I don’t look at a Level II or the time and sales window. Never found it helpful or needed. If anything, it was distraction. My trading is better without it. David · Reply June 1, 2021 at 9:22 PM Excellent info, definitely seems faster paced than waiting for an EMA. Thank you! Leave a Reply Leave a well-reasoned comment or question. Cancel Reply Save my name, email, and website in this browser for the next time I comment. Sign Up for My Free Weekly Trading Tips Newsletter Search the Site Trading Courses © 2026 · TradeThatSwing.  \r\nThis site may profit from affiliate marketing and advertising.\r\nPowered by KZS This website uses cookies to improve your experience. We'll assume you're ok with this, but you can opt-out if you wish. Cookie settings ACCEPT Privacy & Cookies Policy Close Privacy Overview This website uses cookies to improve your experience while you navigate through the website. Out of these cookies, the cookies that are categorized as necessary are stored on your browser as they are essential for the working of basic functionalities of the website. We also use third-party cookies that help us analyze and understand how you use this website. These cookies will be stored in your browser only with your consent. You also have the option to opt-out of these cookies. But opting out of some of these cookies may have an effect on your browsing experience. Necessary Necessary Always Enabled Necessary cookies are absolutely essential for the website to function properly. This category only includes cookies that ensures basic functionalities and security features of the website. These cookies do not store any personal information. Non-necessary Non-necessary Any cookies that may not be particularly necessary for the website to function and is used specifically to collect 